# Runtime and HTTP

Generated dispatch in `rocci-cli` (`dispatch.rs`) plus lowering in
`rocci-template`. Custom `main.roc` apps skip this dispatcher. Generated
apps and the custom datastar/snake examples pin the in-tree Rocci
platform (`crates/rocci-platform`) as `pf` and import `pf.Datastar` /
`pf.Html`. `--http-module` still compiles against basic-webserver 0.16.0.
Wasm apply stays `crates/rocci-roc-host`.

The private actor experiment under `examples/rocci/remote/live-counter`
uses an independent core-Wasm value ABI, shared `CounterCore.roc` decisions,
and compiled Rocci components inside a Cloudflare Durable Object. Its
TypeScript host owns Access authentication, SQLite transactions and operation
receipts, durability barriers, and live delivery. This is an experimental
example adapter, with its own runbook and toolchain pin; it adds no CLI target
or template declaration. Hosted deployment requires owner account setup and
separate acceptance evidence.

## Handler matrix

| Declaration | Method | Success | Datastar | Ordinary client | Route |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `@get:view(path)` | GET | `text/html` document | load/navigation | same | authored path |
| `@get:fragment(path)` | GET | fragment | finite `datastar-patch-elements` | same SSE body | authored path |
| `@post:fragment(path)` and mutation variants | POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE | fragment | finite `datastar-patch-elements` | same SSE body | authored path |
| `@post:command(path)` and mutation variants | POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE | `{}` | finite empty SSE, no morph | `204 No Content` | authored path |
| `@get:live(path)` | GET | fragment | poll stream; skip unchanged HTML | event-stream | authored path |

The closed matrix rejects GET commands and non-GET views/live routes.
Duplicate method/path pairs conflict across the assembled app.
Discriminate Datastar with `Datastar-Request: true`, not `Accept`.
Successful commands have no JSON representation: Datastar receives HTTP
200 finite empty SSE; an ordinary client receives HTTP 204.

Complete examples: [Handler matrix](/examples/handler-matrix/).

## Request access

Generated calls are `handler!(context, request)`. Read the body, headers,
and route data from `request` when the handler lists that parameter.

## Standalone lifecycle

1. `@init` — failure exits the process
2. Listen on loopback (`rocci.toml` / `--port`)
3. Own state in `@context` (typically SQLite)
4. Dispatch normalized document / fragment / command / live routes
5. `?` in handlers → HTTP 500 (HTML overlay for Datastar; error representation otherwise)
6. Shutdown with the process

## Live streams

Every `@get:live(path)` becomes an SSE route. That GET is a long-lived
EventSource. Generated live is a timer poll: each connection re-renders
every 100 ms and emits `datastar-patch-elements` only when HTML bytes
change. A keepalive is a separate unnamed SSE frame so the host 30s idle
timeout does not close a quiet stream. It must not run at poll rate.

A document receives an automatic `data-init=@get(path,
[OpenWhenHidden(True)])` only when its module has exactly one live route
and the body has no authored `data-init`. With multiple streams,
subscribe explicitly.

The live-counter document is now a shared pure `CounterDocument.rocci` module
and subscribes explicitly. The native example reads count and feed from one
SQLite transaction and applies each command's count/feed update atomically.
Its site-island component and native action URLs stay compatible. The actor
adapter renders on committed mutations and active-view clock ticks (once a
minute), with a separate 15-second heartbeat and a current full snapshot on
reconnect. Native generated live streams retain their existing polling policy.

## Datastar asset

Pinned JS is staged under `/assets`. Version comes from
`[assets].datastar` or `rocci datastar pin`.

## Assets, health, logging

`/assets` serves application files. Generated `/health` exists for smoke
checks. `pf.Stderr.line!` is teed to the CLI and Dev Console under
`rocci run`. Do not log from `@component`. `@log` is **planned**.

Environment: `ROC_BASIC_WEBSERVER_PORT`, `ROC_BASIC_WEBSERVER_HOST`
(container override; not a `rocci.toml` field), `DB_PATH` in examples,
`ROCCI_CONFIG`.

## Ordinary Worker handler preview

`rocci worker build` compiles ordinary effectful handlers using a separate
core-Wasm platform. HTML, contexts, SQL codecs, components and route policy
reuse the native path. This workflow requires no Actor extraction or app JS.
Its compiler pin is separate from native `.roc-version`.

The initial Cloudflare profile offers singleton object SQL with exact I64,
explicit transactions, bounded body reads, bounded outbound HTTP and polling
SSE. Finite fragments, empty commands and live GETs retain their separate
roles. Commit and published events await the provider durability barrier.
Transaction handles roll back on final-reference drop. Fetch, body and timer
effects are rejected inside an open transaction. No arbitrary command retry
or exactly once external-effect guarantee is added.

Limits are 32 queued invocations, 1,024 resources, 128 streams, 1 MiB request
bodies/SQL materialization/SSE events, 256 KiB fetch responses and 30-second
async deadlines. Use `Http.send_with!` with an explicit response bound; native
8 MiB HTTP defaults fail. SQL accepts one named-parameter statement and default
open/query options; no ATTACH, raw transaction SQL or duplicate result columns.
Metadata validation happens at execution. Reachable native OS effects fail
compilation. Provider owns listen/lifecycle settings; native Server socket,
pool and shutdown settings do not control managed hosting. Traps/cancellation
discard the generation; an unsettled continuation cannot be reused.

Actual hosted JSPI qualification and local C0/C1/C2 conformance are recorded
separately from release publication. See the
[Worker guide](/docs/applications/workers/) and the platform README for the
current capability boundary.

Finite responses are bounded to 1 MiB. Request targets are bounded to 8 KiB,
headers to 32 KiB and 100 fields. Smaller native Server body/header/target/SSE
limits are honored; incompatible larger limits fail initialization. Provider
listener and pool settings retain the deployment semantics described above.
